const INTERVIEW_BANKS = {
  cs1: {
    code: "CS1",
    name: "Actuarial Statistics",
    note: "100 interview questions ranked by how often they're asked in UK GI actuarial interviews. Cover each answer and say yours aloud in under 60 seconds. Questions 1–20 should be flawless.",
    tiers: [
      { name: "Tier 1: Asked in almost every technical interview", items: [
        {q:"1. Explain a GLM to a non-technical person.", a:"A GLM is a model that looks at each characteristic of a policyholder, such as age, location or type of car, and estimates how much each one raises or lowers expected claims. These effects are then combined, usually by multiplying them together, to produce a fair price for each customer. It is essentially a structured, data-driven way of building a rating table."},
        {q:"2. What are the three components of a GLM?", a:"1) A random component: the response follows a distribution from the exponential family (Normal, Poisson, Binomial, Gamma, Inverse Gaussian). 2) A systematic component: the linear predictor, eta = b0 + b1x1 + ... + bkxk. 3) A link function g that connects the mean to the linear predictor, g(mu) = eta."},
        {q:"3. Which distributions would you use for claim frequency and severity?", a:"Frequency: Poisson with a log link and log(exposure) as an offset; Negative Binomial if overdispersion is present. Severity: Gamma with a log link is the market standard; Lognormal or Inverse Gaussian are alternatives for heavier tails. Large losses are often capped and modelled separately. Pure premium can be modelled directly with a Tweedie distribution."},
        {q:"4. Why use a log link in pricing?", a:"The log link makes the model multiplicative: exp of each coefficient becomes a relativity, matching how rating tables work (base rate x age factor x area factor). It also guarantees predicted means are positive, which is essential for counts and claim amounts. This is why it is used for Gamma severity even though the canonical Gamma link is the inverse."},
        {q:"5. Why not use ordinary linear regression for insurance claims?", a:"Linear regression assumes normally distributed errors with constant variance. Claim counts are discrete and non-negative, claim amounts are positive and right-skewed, and in both cases the variance increases with the mean. Linear regression can also predict negative values. GLMs relax these assumptions by allowing other distributions and a link function."},
        {q:"6. What is a p-value?", a:"The p-value is the probability of observing a test statistic at least as extreme as the one obtained, assuming the null hypothesis is true. A small p-value (e.g. below 5%) indicates the data are unlikely under the null, so we reject it. It is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true, and it says nothing about the size or importance of an effect."},
        {q:"7. Explain Type I and Type II errors with an insurance example.", a:"A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis: for example, concluding a rating factor is significant when it is not, which adds noise and may price customers unfairly. A Type II error is failing to reject a false null: for example, missing a genuine increase in claim frequency, leading to underpricing and losses. The significance level is P(Type I); power is 1 - P(Type II). Reducing one usually increases the other unless sample size increases."},
        {q:"8. What is the difference between correlation and causation?", a:"Correlation means two variables move together; causation means one directly affects the other. Correlation can arise from a confounding variable (e.g. ice cream sales and drownings both rise in summer), reverse causality or chance. In pricing, a factor can be predictive without being causal, but understanding causation matters for fairness, regulation and stability over time."},
        {q:"9. What is overfitting and how do you avoid it?", a:"Overfitting is when a model captures random noise in the training data rather than the underlying pattern, so it performs well in-sample but poorly on new data. Avoid it by keeping models parsimonious, using AIC/BIC or adjusted R-squared, grouping sparse factor levels, applying regularisation, and validating on holdout or cross-validation data."},
        {q:"10. Explain maximum likelihood estimation.", a:"MLE chooses the parameter values that make the observed data most probable. Steps: write the likelihood as the product of densities for each observation, take logs to get the log-likelihood, differentiate with respect to each parameter, set to zero and solve, then check it is a maximum. Where there is no closed form, numerical methods are used, as in GLMs."},
        {q:"11. What does a 95% confidence interval mean?", a:"If we repeatedly drew samples and built an interval the same way each time, about 95% of those intervals would contain the true parameter. The parameter is fixed; the interval is random. It does not mean there is a 95% probability that the true value lies in this particular interval; that interpretation belongs to a Bayesian credible interval."},
        {q:"12. What is the Central Limit Theorem and why does it matter to insurers?", a:"The CLT states that the sum or mean of a large number of independent, identically distributed random variables with finite variance is approximately normally distributed, whatever the original distribution. For insurers, it means total claims from a large portfolio are relatively predictable: the standard deviation grows with the square root of n while the mean grows with n, so relative risk falls. This is the statistical basis of risk pooling."},
        {q:"13. What is credibility theory?", a:"Credibility theory blends a risk's own claims experience with a broader collateral estimate: Premium = Z x (own experience) + (1 - Z) x (collateral estimate), where Z is the credibility factor between 0 and 1. It is used when a risk has some data but not enough to rely on fully, such as a small fleet or a new scheme."},
        {q:"14. What makes the credibility factor Z increase?", a:"Z increases with: more data from the risk itself (more years or more exposure); lower volatility in the risk's own experience; and greater heterogeneity between risks, which makes the collateral data less relevant. Z should lie between 0 and 1 and tend to 1 as the volume of own data grows."},
        {q:"15. Interpret a coefficient of 0.3 in a log-link GLM.", a:"Under a log link, exp(0.3) = 1.35. So, holding all other factors constant, policies with that characteristic have about 35% higher expected claims (frequency or severity, depending on the model) than the base level. A negative coefficient, e.g. -0.2, gives exp(-0.2) = 0.82, an 18% reduction."},
        {q:"16. What is an offset in a frequency GLM?", a:"An offset is a term in the linear predictor with its coefficient fixed at 1. In a frequency model we include log(exposure) as an offset so that log(expected claims) = log(exposure) + linear predictor, meaning expected claims are proportional to time on risk. This lets us model claim rates per unit exposure while using claim counts as the response."},
        {q:"17. How do you decide whether to keep a rating factor?", a:"Statistically: significance of coefficients, standard errors, deviance or chi-squared tests, and AIC. Consistency: the effect should be stable across years and in holdout data. Business sense: the direction and size should be explainable. Regulation: factors like gender are banned in UK pricing and price-walking rules apply. Practicality: the data must be reliably collected at the point of sale."},
        {q:"18. What is overdispersion and how do you deal with it?", a:"In a Poisson model the variance equals the mean. Overdispersion occurs when the observed variance is larger, often because policyholders differ in underlying risk. It causes standard errors to be understated and factors to look falsely significant. Remedies: quasi-Poisson (estimating a dispersion parameter), or a Negative Binomial model."},
        {q:"19. How do you compare two models?", a:"For nested models, use the difference in scaled deviance, which follows a chi-squared distribution with degrees of freedom equal to the difference in parameters (or an F-test if the scale parameter is estimated). For non-nested models, use AIC or BIC. In practice also compare holdout performance, lift charts, actual versus expected and residual diagnostics."},
        {q:"20. Which CS1 topic is most useful in general insurance and why?", a:"GLMs, because they are the industry standard for personal lines pricing and are also used in claims and retention modelling. Credibility is a close second for commercial and scheme pricing. A strong answer links this to a project where you fitted a GLM in R, interpreted relativities and validated the model."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 2: Very common follow-ups", items: [
        {q:"21. What is deviance?", a:"Deviance measures how far a model is from a perfect fit. D = 2[l(saturated) - l(fitted)], where the saturated model has one parameter per observation. Lower deviance means better fit. Scaled deviance divides by the dispersion parameter and is approximately chi-squared, which allows testing between nested models."},
        {q:"22. What is AIC?", a:"Akaike Information Criterion: AIC = -2 x log-likelihood + 2p, where p is the number of parameters. It rewards goodness of fit but penalises complexity. Lower AIC is better. It can compare non-nested models fitted to the same data."},
        {q:"23. What is the difference between AIC and BIC?", a:"BIC = -2 x log-likelihood + p ln(n). Because ln(n) exceeds 2 once n is above about 7, BIC penalises extra parameters more heavily and favours simpler models, especially with large datasets typical in insurance."},
        {q:"24. What is the exponential family?", a:"A family of distributions whose density can be written f(y) = exp[(y theta - b(theta)) / phi + c(y, phi)]. theta is the natural parameter and phi the scale parameter. Mean = b'(theta) and variance = phi b''(theta). Normal, Poisson, Binomial, Gamma and Inverse Gaussian all belong to it, which is what allows a unified GLM framework."},
        {q:"25. What is a canonical link?", a:"The link function for which g(mu) equals the natural parameter theta. Normal: identity. Poisson: log. Binomial: logit. Gamma: inverse. Canonical links have nice mathematical properties, but in practice the log link is often chosen for business reasons."},
        {q:"26. What residuals do you use to check a GLM?", a:"Pearson residuals: (y - mu) / sqrt(V(mu)). Deviance residuals: sign(y - mu) x sqrt of each observation's deviance contribution, which tend to be closer to normal. Plot them against fitted values and each factor; patterns suggest a missing variable, wrong link or wrong variance function."},
        {q:"27. What are the assumptions of linear regression?", a:"Linearity between response and predictors; independent errors; constant error variance (homoscedasticity); errors normally distributed with mean zero. Also no perfect multicollinearity. Check with residual plots, Q-Q plots and leverage diagnostics."},
        {q:"28. What does R-squared measure?", a:"The proportion of total variability in the response explained by the model: R-squared = SSR / SST = 1 - SSE / SST. It ranges from 0 to 1. A high R-squared does not prove the model is correct or causal."},
        {q:"29. Why use adjusted R-squared?", a:"R-squared never decreases when a variable is added, even a useless one. Adjusted R-squared = 1 - (1 - R-squared)(n - 1)/(n - p - 1) penalises extra parameters, so it only increases if the new variable improves the model more than expected by chance."},
        {q:"30. What is multicollinearity?", a:"When explanatory variables are highly correlated with each other, e.g. vehicle value and engine size. Coefficient estimates become unstable with inflated standard errors and may have counterintuitive signs, though predictions may still be fine. Detect with correlation matrices or variance inflation factors; fix by removing, combining or regularising variables."},
        {q:"31. What is an interaction term?", a:"A term allowing the effect of one variable to depend on another. For example, the effect of driver age on claims may differ between sports cars and family cars. Without the interaction the model assumes the age effect is the same for all vehicles."},
        {q:"32. What is the key property of the Poisson distribution?", a:"Mean equals variance, both equal to lambda. It models the number of events in a fixed period when events occur independently at a constant rate. The sum of independent Poissons is Poisson."},
        {q:"33. Why might the Negative Binomial fit claim counts better than the Poisson?", a:"Policyholders differ in their underlying risk. If each policyholder's claim count is Poisson with a mean that itself follows a Gamma distribution, the resulting mixed distribution is Negative Binomial. It has variance greater than the mean, capturing overdispersion seen in real data."},
        {q:"34. Which distributions have heavy tails?", a:"Pareto, Lognormal, Weibull with shape parameter below 1, Burr and Log-gamma. Heavy-tailed distributions give a higher probability of extreme values; some (Pareto) may not have finite higher moments. They are important for large losses, liability and reinsurance."},
        {q:"35. Why use a Pareto distribution for large losses?", a:"Its tail decays polynomially rather than exponentially, so it assigns realistic probabilities to very large claims. An exponential or Gamma would underestimate the frequency of extreme losses, leading to underpriced excess-of-loss reinsurance or insufficient capital."},
        {q:"36. What are the mean and variance of aggregate claims?", a:"For S = X1 + ... + XN, with Xi iid and independent of N: E(S) = E(N)E(X) and Var(S) = E(N)Var(X) + Var(N)[E(X)]^2. The first variance term is uncertainty in claim sizes; the second is uncertainty in claim numbers."},
        {q:"37. What is the variance of a compound Poisson distribution?", a:"Var(S) = lambda E(X^2), since E(N) = Var(N) = lambda. The mean is lambda E(X). A useful property: the sum of independent compound Poisson variables is also compound Poisson."},
        {q:"38. When does the Central Limit Theorem fail or work poorly?", a:"Small samples; highly skewed distributions where convergence is slow; heavy-tailed distributions with infinite variance; and dependent risks, such as a windstorm affecting many properties at once. In these cases a normal approximation will understate tail risk."},
        {q:"39. What is the difference between Bayesian and frequentist approaches?", a:"Frequentists treat parameters as fixed unknown constants and data as random; inference is based on repeated sampling. Bayesians treat parameters as random variables with a prior distribution reflecting existing beliefs, updated with data via Bayes' theorem to give a posterior: posterior is proportional to likelihood x prior."},
        {q:"40. What is a conjugate prior?", a:"A prior that gives a posterior from the same family, making updating simple. Examples: Beta prior with Binomial data gives Beta posterior; Gamma prior with Poisson data gives Gamma posterior; Normal prior with Normal data (known variance) gives Normal posterior; Gamma prior with Exponential data gives Gamma posterior."},
        {q:"41. What is the difference between a confidence interval and a credible interval?", a:"A Bayesian credible interval is taken from the posterior distribution and can be read directly: there is a 95% probability the parameter lies within it, given data and prior. A frequentist confidence interval is about the long-run behaviour of the method, not a probability for this specific interval."},
        {q:"42. What is bootstrapping?", a:"A resampling method: draw many samples with replacement from the observed data (non-parametric) or simulate from a fitted model (parametric), compute the statistic each time, and use the resulting distribution to estimate standard errors or confidence intervals. In GI it is widely used to estimate reserve variability, e.g. bootstrapping the chain ladder."},
        {q:"43. What is the difference between statistical and practical significance?", a:"Statistical significance means an effect is unlikely to be due to chance. Practical significance means it is large enough to matter commercially. With millions of policies even a 0.5% effect can be statistically significant but not worth adding to a rating structure."},
        {q:"44. What is the difference between Pearson and Spearman correlation?", a:"Pearson measures the strength of a linear relationship and is sensitive to outliers. Spearman is Pearson applied to ranks, measuring any monotonic relationship and robust to outliers. Kendall's tau is another rank measure based on concordant and discordant pairs."},
        {q:"45. Claims rose this year. How would you test if the increase is real?", a:"First check data and exposure: has the portfolio grown or changed mix? Are there reporting or system changes? Then compare claim frequency (claims per exposure) using a Poisson-based test or include a year factor in a GLM controlling for mix. Assess whether the change is statistically and practically significant, and look for external drivers like inflation or weather events."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 3: Asked by technical or pricing-heavy interviewers", items: [
        {q:"46. What are the properties of maximum likelihood estimators?", a:"Under regularity conditions MLEs are asymptotically unbiased, asymptotically normal, asymptotically efficient (variance approaches the Cramer-Rao lower bound) and consistent. They are also invariant: if theta-hat is the MLE of theta, then g(theta-hat) is the MLE of g(theta). They can be biased in small samples."},
        {q:"47. Why do we take logs of the likelihood?", a:"The likelihood is a product of densities; taking logs turns it into a sum, which is far easier to differentiate. Because the log function is strictly increasing, the log-likelihood is maximised at the same parameter values. It also avoids numerical underflow with many observations."},
        {q:"48. Compare the method of moments with maximum likelihood.", a:"Method of moments equates sample moments to population moments and solves for parameters; it is simple and quick but can be inefficient and give impossible values. MLE is generally more efficient and has better large-sample properties, but may need numerical optimisation. Method of moments estimates are often used as starting values for MLE."},
        {q:"49. What are bias and mean squared error?", a:"Bias = E(estimator) - true parameter. MSE = E[(estimator - parameter)^2] = variance + bias^2. MSE balances accuracy and precision, so a slightly biased estimator with much smaller variance can be preferred."},
        {q:"50. What is a consistent estimator?", a:"An estimator that converges in probability to the true parameter as the sample size increases. A sufficient condition is that both bias and variance tend to zero as n tends to infinity."},
        {q:"51. What is the Cramer-Rao lower bound?", a:"The lowest possible variance for an unbiased estimator: Var(theta-hat) >= 1 / (n I(theta)), where I(theta) is the Fisher information per observation, -E[second derivative of log f]. An unbiased estimator attaining it is efficient. It is also used to give approximate variances and confidence intervals for MLEs."},
        {q:"52. Why does the sample variance divide by n - 1?", a:"Using the sample mean instead of the true mean makes the squared deviations systematically smaller. Dividing by n - 1 corrects this so that E(s^2) = sigma^2, making s^2 unbiased. Intuitively, one degree of freedom is used in estimating the mean."},
        {q:"53. When do you use the t distribution instead of the Normal?", a:"When the population is normal, the variance is unknown and must be estimated by the sample variance, and the sample is small. (X-bar - mu)/(s/sqrt(n)) then follows a t distribution with n - 1 degrees of freedom. For large samples the t approaches the Normal."},
        {q:"54. What is the chi-squared goodness of fit test?", a:"It tests whether observed data follow a hypothesised distribution. Statistic: sum of (O - E)^2 / E over categories, approximately chi-squared with (number of cells - 1 - number of parameters estimated) degrees of freedom. Expected frequencies should be at least 5, otherwise combine cells. Example: testing whether claim counts are Poisson."},
        {q:"55. What is a contingency table test used for?", a:"Testing whether two categorical variables are independent, e.g. whether making a claim is independent of region. Expected count = row total x column total / grand total. Degrees of freedom = (rows - 1)(columns - 1)."},
        {q:"56. What affects the width of a confidence interval?", a:"Sample size (larger n gives narrower intervals, width proportional to 1/sqrt(n)); variability in the data (more variability gives wider intervals); and the confidence level (99% is wider than 95%)."},
        {q:"57. How can you increase the power of a test?", a:"Increase sample size, test for a larger true effect, reduce variability (e.g. better measurement or paired designs), increase the significance level, or use a one-tailed test when the direction is known in advance."},
        {q:"58. What is the difference between one-tailed and two-tailed tests?", a:"A one-tailed test looks for a difference in one specified direction (has frequency increased?); a two-tailed test looks for any difference. One-tailed tests have more power in the specified direction but cannot detect effects in the other direction, so the choice must be made before seeing the data."},
        {q:"59. What is the multiple testing problem?", a:"Each test at 5% has a 5% chance of a false positive, so running many tests makes at least one false positive likely. Testing 20 rating factors almost guarantees a spurious one. Remedies include the Bonferroni correction (divide the significance level by the number of tests) and validating findings on separate data."},
        {q:"60. What is the difference between independence and zero correlation?", a:"Independence means the joint distribution equals the product of the marginals; it implies zero correlation. Zero correlation only rules out a linear relationship. Example: if X is symmetric about zero and Y = X^2, they are uncorrelated but clearly dependent. The exception is the bivariate normal, where zero correlation implies independence."},
        {q:"61. State the laws of total expectation and variance.", a:"E(X) = E[E(X|Y)]. Var(X) = E[Var(X|Y)] + Var[E(X|Y)]. The first variance term is the average within-group variability; the second is between group means. These underpin compound distributions and credibility theory."},
        {q:"62. What is a moment generating function?", a:"M(t) = E(e^(tX)). The rth derivative at t = 0 gives E(X^r). It uniquely identifies a distribution, and for independent variables the MGF of a sum equals the product of the MGFs, which makes it powerful for sums and aggregate claims."},
        {q:"63. Does every distribution have an MGF?", a:"No. For the Pareto and Lognormal, E(e^(tX)) is infinite for any t > 0, because their tails are too heavy. Absence of an MGF is a signal of a heavy-tailed distribution, which is exactly the kind used for large insurance losses."},
        {q:"64. What is a cumulant generating function?", a:"C(t) = ln M(t). Its first derivative at zero gives the mean, the second gives the variance and the third gives the third central moment, from which the coefficient of skewness is calculated. It simplifies calculations for sums of independent variables because cumulants add."},
        {q:"65. What is the memoryless property?", a:"For the exponential distribution, P(X > s + t | X > s) = P(X > t): having already waited s units tells you nothing about the remaining wait. The geometric is the discrete equivalent. It makes the exponential natural for times between claims in a Poisson process."},
        {q:"66. Why are claim amounts usually positively skewed?", a:"Most claims are small (windscreens, minor damage) but a few are very large (total losses, injury claims), producing a long right tail. As a result the mean exceeds the median and a small number of claims drive a large share of total cost."},
        {q:"67. What Bayesian estimators arise from different loss functions?", a:"Quadratic (squared error) loss gives the posterior mean. Absolute error loss gives the posterior median. All-or-nothing (zero-one) loss gives the posterior mode."},
        {q:"68. What is the credibility factor in the Poisson/Gamma model?", a:"With Poisson claims and a Gamma(alpha, lambda) prior on the mean, the posterior mean is Z x-bar + (1 - Z) alpha/lambda with Z = n/(n + lambda). The Bayesian estimate is therefore exactly a credibility estimate, with Z rising as n grows. For Normal/Normal, Z = n/(n + sigma^2/sigma0^2)."},
        {q:"69. What is the difference between EBCT Model 1 and Model 2?", a:"Model 1 (Buhlmann) assumes each risk has the same volume in every year. Model 2 (Buhlmann-Straub) allows different risk volumes (e.g. number of policies or exposure) in each year and across risks, so it is more realistic for actual portfolios."},
        {q:"70. Interpret E[s^2(theta)] and Var[m(theta)] in EBCT.", a:"E[s^2(theta)] is the average variance of a risk's claims from year to year (process variance, noise within a risk). Var[m(theta)] is the variance of the true underlying means across risks (heterogeneity). When risks are very different from each other, Var[m(theta)] is high and Z increases; when individual experience is noisy, E[s^2(theta)] is high and Z falls."},
        {q:"71. What are the problems with Bayesian credibility?", a:"It requires specifying both a prior distribution for the risk parameter and a distribution for the claims, which may be hard to justify or estimate. Results can be sensitive to these choices. Empirical Bayes Credibility Theory addresses this by estimating the required parameters from data with fewer distributional assumptions."},
        {q:"72. What is the difference between a confidence interval and a prediction interval in regression?", a:"A confidence interval estimates the mean response at a given x and reflects only parameter uncertainty. A prediction interval is for a single new observation and also includes the random error term, so it is always wider."},
        {q:"73. How do you handle non-linear relationships?", a:"Transform variables (log, square root), add polynomial terms, use splines, or band a continuous variable into groups (common in pricing, e.g. driver age bands). A GLM with a suitable link can also capture non-linearity on the original scale."},
        {q:"74. How do you choose the base level for a categorical factor?", a:"Choose the level with the most exposure. This gives the most stable intercept and the smallest standard errors for the other levels' relativities, making significance tests more meaningful."},
        {q:"75. What are the limitations of GLMs?", a:"They assume a specific distribution and variance function, a linear predictor on the link scale, and independent observations. Complex non-linearities and interactions must be specified by the modeller. They are sensitive to data quality and can overfit sparse factor levels. Machine learning methods such as gradient boosting can capture more structure, but GLMs remain preferred for transparency and regulatory explainability."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 4: Data analysis, practical and scenario questions", items: [
        {q:"76. What are the main types of data?", a:"Numerical: discrete (claim counts) or continuous (claim amounts). Categorical: nominal with no order (vehicle make, region) or ordinal with an order (no-claims-discount level). Data can also be classed as cross-sectional, time series or longitudinal (panel)."},
        {q:"77. Descriptive, inferential and predictive analysis: what is the difference?", a:"Descriptive analysis summarises what has happened (average claim, loss ratio). Inferential analysis draws conclusions about a population from a sample (is London frequency significantly higher?). Predictive analysis forecasts future outcomes (expected claims for next year's policies)."},
        {q:"78. What are the key stages of a data analysis process?", a:"Define the objectives; collect data; clean and process it; carry out exploratory data analysis; model; communicate results clearly; and monitor and update. Throughout, document assumptions and check data quality."},
        {q:"79. How do you handle missing data?", a:"Understand why it is missing (random or systematic). Options: remove records if few and random; impute using means, medians or models; or create a separate 'unknown' category, which in pricing can itself be predictive. Document the approach and test sensitivity."},
        {q:"80. How do you treat outliers in claims data?", a:"First check whether they are errors (e.g. a misplaced decimal) or genuine large losses. Errors are corrected or removed. Genuine large claims should not be deleted; they are typically capped at a threshold in the GLM, and the excess cost is loaded back across the portfolio or priced separately."},
        {q:"81. What is reproducible research and why does it matter?", a:"Research where another analyst can rerun the work and get the same results. It requires documented code, version control, recorded data sources and fixed random seeds. It supports peer review, audit, regulatory scrutiny and reduces errors."},
        {q:"82. What risks are associated with using data?", a:"Poor data quality; bias or unrepresentative samples; historic data not reflecting the future (e.g. inflation or pandemic effects); data protection and GDPR obligations; and ethical or discrimination concerns from proxy variables."},
        {q:"83. What is big data?", a:"Datasets characterised by high volume, velocity (speed of generation), variety (structured and unstructured) and veracity (uncertain quality). Examples in insurance: telematics, smart home sensors and online quote data."},
        {q:"84. How would you check data quality?", a:"Reconcile totals to finance or accounts; check ranges and impossible values; look for duplicates and missing fields; check consistency over time and between related fields (e.g. claim date after policy start); and discuss anomalies with data owners."},
        {q:"85. How do you validate a pricing model?", a:"Test it on holdout or out-of-time data. Use lift charts and Gini coefficients to assess discrimination, actual versus expected by each factor and by segment, residual analysis, and check stability of relativities across years. Also review with underwriters for business sense."},
        {q:"86. What is a lift chart?", a:"Policies are ranked by predicted cost and grouped into bands (e.g. deciles). Average predicted and actual cost are plotted for each band. A good model shows a steep, well-matched line, meaning it separates low and high risks accurately."},
        {q:"87. How would you explain variance to a manager?", a:"Variance measures how much results tend to swing around the average. Two portfolios can have the same average claim cost, but the one with higher variance is less predictable and needs more capital or reinsurance."},
        {q:"88. How would you explain a p-value to an underwriter?", a:"It tells us how surprising our data would be if nothing had actually changed. A very small p-value means the pattern would be very unlikely by chance, so we believe the effect is real."},
        {q:"89. How would you price a small scheme with three years of good experience?", a:"Three years from a small scheme is not fully credible, as good results may be luck. Use credibility: blend the scheme's own loss experience with the book or market rate, with Z reflecting the volume and stability of its data. Also adjust for trends, inflation, IBNR and any changes in the scheme's risk profile."},
        {q:"90. Claims fell 15% after a rule change. Is the effect real?", a:"Check whether exposure or business mix changed, whether claims are simply not yet reported (IBNR, especially for recent periods), and whether seasonality or weather explains it. Then test significance with a Poisson test or GLM including a before/after factor. Monitor for a longer period before concluding."},
        {q:"91. Walk me through pricing a motor insurance product.", a:"Gather and clean policy and claims data, matching exposure to claims. Explore the data. Fit a Poisson GLM for frequency with a log(exposure) offset and a Gamma GLM for severity, capping large losses. Select and band rating factors using statistical tests, AIC and business judgement. Validate on holdout data. Combine frequency x severity for the risk premium, apply credibility where data are thin, then add loadings for large losses, expenses, reinsurance, commission and profit, and finally consider competitor and regulatory constraints."},
        {q:"92. Gender cannot be used in UK pricing. What does this mean statistically?", a:"The factor must be removed even if predictive. Other correlated variables (occupation, vehicle type) may then absorb some of its effect, acting as proxies. Firms must monitor for indirect discrimination and treat customers fairly under FCA rules such as Consumer Duty."},
        {q:"93. How do GLMs compare with machine learning?", a:"Machine learning methods (gradient boosting, random forests) can capture complex non-linearities and interactions automatically and often predict slightly better. GLMs are transparent, produce clear relativities, are easier to implement in rating engines and easier to explain to regulators and underwriters. Many insurers use ML to find patterns and then build them into GLMs."},
        {q:"94. What is the Tweedie distribution and when is it used?", a:"A compound Poisson-Gamma distribution with a point mass at zero and a continuous positive part. It models pure premium (cost per unit exposure) directly in one GLM, which suits insurance data where most policies have no claims."},
        {q:"95. What software have you used for statistical analysis?", a:"Name tools honestly, e.g. R for GLMs and data analysis, plus Excel, and Python or SQL if applicable. Give a concrete example of something you built with each, such as fitting a GLM in R for an MSc project."},
        {q:"96. Describe a statistical project you have done.", a:"Use a structure: aim, data, method, result, what you learned. Example: 'In my MSc at Kent I built a claims model in R. I cleaned the data, fitted Poisson and Gamma GLMs, selected factors using deviance tests and AIC, and validated with actual versus expected. The biggest lesson was how much time data cleaning takes and how important business sense is in factor selection.'"},
        {q:"97. How would you explain a surprising model result to a senior colleague?", a:"First check the data and code for errors. If it holds up, explain it in plain language with supporting evidence (charts, exposure behind the result), state the limitations and uncertainty, and suggest next steps such as further investigation or monitoring."},
        {q:"98. Which CS1 topic did you find hardest and how did you master it?", a:"Be honest and specific, e.g. Empirical Bayes Credibility Theory, because it combines several ideas. Explain how you overcame it: working through past papers, building intuition about within versus between variance, and practising calculations until the structure was clear."},
        {q:"99. How does CS1 link to later exams and work?", a:"CS1 provides probability, estimation, hypothesis testing, regression, GLMs and credibility. CS2 extends this to time series, survival models, copulas, extreme value theory and machine learning. CP and SP exams apply these tools to real commercial problems such as pricing and reserving."},
        {q:"100. If you had more data, how would you improve a model?", a:"Add new predictive variables (telematics, external data such as weather or crime rates), test interactions, refine banding, increase credibility of thin segments, and improve validation using out-of-time testing. Always weigh gains against complexity, data quality and regulatory considerations."}
      ]}
    ]
  },
  cb1: {
    code: "CB1",
    name: "Business Finance",
    note: "100 interview questions ranked by how often they're asked in UK actuarial analyst interviews. Cover each answer and say yours aloud in under 60 seconds. Questions 1–20 should be flawless. Questions 15, 16, 71, 72 and 73 are the GI differentiators.",
    tiers: [
      { name: "Tier 1: Asked in almost every interview", items: [
        {q:"1. What are the three main financial statements?", a:"The income statement (profit and loss) shows revenue, expenses and profit over a period. The statement of financial position (balance sheet) shows assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time. The cash flow statement shows cash generated and used in operating, investing and financing activities."},
        {q:"2. What is the difference between profit and cash flow?", a:"Profit is measured on an accruals basis: income and costs are recognised when earned or incurred, and includes non-cash items like depreciation. Cash flow is actual money in and out. A profitable company can fail through lack of cash, which is why liquidity matters."},
        {q:"3. What is the accruals (matching) concept?", a:"Revenue and expenses are recognised in the period they relate to, not when cash changes hands. For insurers, premiums are earned over the policy period, which is why an unearned premium reserve is held."},
        {q:"4. Explain the balance sheet equation.", a:"Assets = Liabilities + Equity. Equity (shareholders' funds) is the residual interest after liabilities are deducted. For an insurer, technical provisions (claims reserves and unearned premiums) are usually the largest liabilities."},
        {q:"5. What is the cost of capital?", a:"The return a company must earn to satisfy its providers of finance. Cost of equity is the return shareholders require; cost of debt is the after-tax interest rate. It is the hurdle rate for investment decisions."},
        {q:"6. What is WACC?", a:"Weighted average cost of capital: the cost of each source of finance weighted by its market value. WACC = (E/V) x cost of equity + (D/V) x cost of debt x (1 - tax rate). Used as the discount rate for projects with similar risk to the company's existing business."},
        {q:"7. Debt vs equity finance: advantages and disadvantages?", a:"Debt is cheaper (lower required return, interest is tax deductible) and does not dilute control, but adds fixed obligations and financial risk. Equity has no compulsory payments and absorbs losses, but is more expensive and dilutes ownership. The right mix balances cost and risk."},
        {q:"8. What is gearing (leverage)?", a:"The proportion of debt in a company's capital structure, e.g. debt / (debt + equity). Higher gearing increases returns to shareholders in good times but magnifies losses and raises the risk of insolvency."},
        {q:"9. What is the Modigliani-Miller theory?", a:"Without taxes, in perfect markets, firm value is independent of capital structure: cheaper debt is offset by a rising cost of equity. With corporate taxes, debt adds value through the interest tax shield, implying high gearing. In reality, bankruptcy costs and agency costs limit optimal gearing (trade-off theory)."},
        {q:"10. How is the cost of equity estimated?", a:"Using the Capital Asset Pricing Model: cost of equity = risk-free rate + beta x (market return - risk-free rate). Alternatively, the dividend growth model: cost of equity = D1/P0 + g."},
        {q:"11. What is beta?", a:"A measure of a share's systematic (market) risk: its sensitivity to market movements. Beta 1 moves with the market, above 1 is more volatile, below 1 less. Only systematic risk is rewarded because specific risk can be diversified away."},
        {q:"12. What are the main sources of finance for a company?", a:"Short-term: overdrafts, trade credit, short-term loans, factoring. Long-term: retained earnings, ordinary shares, preference shares, debentures and bonds, bank loans, leasing. Insurers also use subordinated debt counting as regulatory capital."},
        {q:"13. What is the difference between ordinary and preference shares?", a:"Ordinary shares carry voting rights and a residual claim on profits and assets, with variable dividends. Preference shares usually pay a fixed dividend paid before ordinary dividends, rank ahead on winding up, and normally have no votes. They sit between debt and equity."},
        {q:"14. What ratios would you use to analyse a company?", a:"Profitability (return on capital employed, margins), liquidity (current ratio, quick ratio), efficiency (asset turnover, receivables days), gearing (debt/equity, interest cover) and investor ratios (EPS, P/E, dividend yield, dividend cover)."},
        {q:"15. What ratios matter most for a general insurer?", a:"Loss ratio (claims / earned premium), expense ratio, combined ratio (loss + expense ratio; below 100% means an underwriting profit), investment return, return on equity and solvency coverage ratio (own funds / SCR)."},
        {q:"16. What is the combined ratio?", a:"Claims incurred plus expenses, divided by earned premium. Below 100% means the insurer makes an underwriting profit before investment income; above 100% means an underwriting loss."},
        {q:"17. What is limited liability?", a:"Shareholders' losses are limited to the amount they invested; personal assets are protected. It encourages investment and allows companies to raise large capital, but creditors bear more risk, so disclosure and regulation are required."},
        {q:"18. What are the main types of business entity?", a:"Sole trader, partnership (including LLP) and company (private limited or public limited). Companies have separate legal personality and limited liability; plcs can raise capital from the public and list shares. Mutuals are owned by their members, common in insurance."},
        {q:"19. What is dividend policy and what affects it?", a:"The decision on how much profit to pay out versus retain. Influences: investment opportunities, liquidity, shareholder expectations, signalling, tax, legal and regulatory restrictions (insurers need PRA solvency headroom) and stability. Companies often prefer stable, gradually rising dividends."},
        {q:"20. Which CB1 topic is most relevant to an actuary?", a:"Understanding insurer financial statements (technical provisions, combined ratio), cost of capital and WACC for setting risk discount rates and capital allocation, and capital structure, as insurers manage solvency capital under Solvency UK."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 2: Very common follow-ups", items: [
        {q:"21. What is depreciation and why is it charged?", a:"The allocation of a non-current asset's cost over its useful life, applying the accruals concept. Methods include straight-line and reducing balance. It is a non-cash expense, so it is added back in the cash flow statement."},
        {q:"22. What is the difference between capital and revenue expenditure?", a:"Capital expenditure buys or improves long-term assets and goes on the balance sheet. Revenue expenditure covers day-to-day running costs and goes through the income statement."},
        {q:"23. What is goodwill?", a:"The excess of the price paid to acquire a business over the fair value of its identifiable net assets, reflecting reputation, customer base and synergies. It is tested annually for impairment under IFRS."},
        {q:"24. What are intangible assets?", a:"Non-physical assets such as goodwill, brands, patents, software and licences. They are recognised if identifiable and measurable, and amortised or tested for impairment."},
        {q:"25. What is working capital?", a:"Current assets minus current liabilities. It funds day-to-day operations. Too little risks liquidity problems; too much ties up cash unproductively."},
        {q:"26. Explain the current ratio and quick ratio.", a:"Current ratio = current assets / current liabilities. Quick (acid test) ratio excludes inventory, which may be hard to sell quickly. Both measure short-term liquidity."},
        {q:"27. What is return on capital employed (ROCE)?", a:"Profit before interest and tax divided by capital employed (equity plus long-term debt). It measures how efficiently a company uses all its long-term capital to generate profit."},
        {q:"28. What is earnings per share and the P/E ratio?", a:"EPS = profit attributable to ordinary shareholders / number of ordinary shares. P/E = share price / EPS, showing how much investors pay per pound of earnings, reflecting growth expectations and risk."},
        {q:"29. What is interest cover?", a:"Profit before interest and tax / interest payable. It shows how comfortably a company can pay its interest; low cover signals financial risk."},
        {q:"30. What are the limitations of ratio analysis?", a:"Based on historical data, affected by accounting policies and window dressing, seasonal distortions, difficulty comparing companies of different sizes or sectors, and it identifies symptoms rather than causes."},
        {q:"31. What is an IPO and what are the ways of issuing shares?", a:"An initial public offering lists a company's shares on a stock exchange for the first time. Methods: offer for sale, placing with institutions, and introduction. Existing listed companies raise more equity through rights issues."},
        {q:"32. What is a rights issue?", a:"An offer to existing shareholders to buy new shares in proportion to their holdings, usually at a discount. It protects their proportion of ownership and is cheaper than a public offer."},
        {q:"33. What is a scrip issue (bonus issue)?", a:"Free new shares issued to existing shareholders from reserves. It raises no cash, increases the number of shares and reduces the share price proportionately, often to improve marketability."},
        {q:"34. What are debentures and bonds?", a:"Long-term debt securities paying interest. They may be secured on assets (fixed or floating charge) or unsecured, and can include covenants restricting the borrower. Convertible bonds can be converted into shares."},
        {q:"35. What is a convertible bond?", a:"A bond the holder can convert into a fixed number of shares. It pays a lower coupon because of the conversion option, lets companies raise cheaper debt, and may later reduce gearing on conversion."},
        {q:"36. What is leasing and why do companies lease?", a:"Using an asset in return for regular payments without owning it. Benefits: conserves cash, flexibility, avoids obsolescence risk. Under IFRS 16 most leases now appear on the balance sheet as right-of-use assets and lease liabilities."},
        {q:"37. What is the difference between corporation tax and income tax?", a:"Corporation tax is charged on company profits; income tax is charged on individuals' income, including dividends. Interest paid by companies is deductible for corporation tax, while dividends are paid from post-tax profits."},
        {q:"38. What is the tax shield on debt?", a:"Because interest is tax deductible, the after-tax cost of debt is interest x (1 - tax rate). This makes debt cheaper than equity and can increase company value."},
        {q:"39. What is the trade-off theory of capital structure?", a:"Companies balance the tax benefits of debt against the costs of financial distress and bankruptcy. Optimal gearing is where the marginal benefit equals the marginal cost."},
        {q:"40. What is the pecking order theory?", a:"Companies prefer internal funds first, then debt, and equity last, because issuing shares signals managers think shares are overvalued and is costly. It explains why profitable firms often have low gearing."},
        {q:"41. What is agency theory?", a:"Conflicts between principals (shareholders) and agents (managers) whose interests differ. Agency costs include monitoring and incentive schemes. Debt can reduce agency costs by disciplining managers."},
        {q:"42. What is corporate governance?", a:"The system by which companies are directed and controlled. Key elements: an effective board, independent non-executive directors, separation of chair and CEO, audit and remuneration committees, transparency and accountability. In the UK, the Corporate Governance Code applies."},
        {q:"43. What is the role of an auditor?", a:"To give an independent opinion on whether the financial statements give a true and fair view and comply with accounting standards. Auditors increase confidence in published accounts."},
        {q:"44. What are the main risks a business faces?", a:"Market risk, credit risk, liquidity risk, operational risk, business and strategic risk, regulatory and legal risk. For insurers add insurance (underwriting and reserving) risk."},
        {q:"45. What is risk management?", a:"Identifying, assessing, controlling and monitoring risks. Responses: avoid, reduce, transfer (insurance, reinsurance, hedging) or accept. Enterprise risk management takes a holistic view across all risks."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 3: Asked by more technical interviewers", items: [
        {q:"46. What are technical provisions for a general insurer?", a:"Liabilities for insurance contracts: claims provisions (reported outstanding claims and IBNR) and premium provisions (unexpired risk on unearned premium). Under Solvency UK they are best estimate plus a risk margin."},
        {q:"47. What is the unearned premium reserve?", a:"The part of written premium relating to cover not yet provided at the accounting date. With annual policies written evenly, it is roughly half the annual written premium."},
        {q:"48. What is IBNR?", a:"Incurred but not reported claims: losses that have happened but have not yet been notified to the insurer. Actuaries estimate IBNR using methods like the chain ladder."},
        {q:"49. Written vs earned premium?", a:"Written premium is premium on policies incepting in the period. Earned premium is the portion relating to cover provided in the period. Earned premium is used in the loss ratio."},
        {q:"50. What is IFRS 17?", a:"The international accounting standard for insurance contracts, effective 2023. It measures contracts as fulfilment cashflows (discounted best estimate plus a risk adjustment) and a contractual service margin for unearned profit. GI short-term contracts often use the simplified premium allocation approach."},
        {q:"51. What is Solvency UK (Solvency II)?", a:"The prudential regime for UK insurers with three pillars: quantitative requirements (technical provisions, SCR, MCR), governance and supervisory review (including ORSA), and disclosure. Solvency UK is the post-Brexit reformed version."},
        {q:"52. What is the SCR?", a:"The solvency capital requirement: capital needed to survive a 1-in-200-year loss over one year (99.5% VaR). Calculated by the standard formula or an approved internal model."},
        {q:"53. What is an ORSA?", a:"Own Risk and Solvency Assessment: the insurer's own forward-looking assessment of its risks and capital needs, linking risk management, business plans and capital."},
        {q:"54. What is the difference between a cash-settled and asset-backed balance sheet for an insurer?", a:"Insurers collect premiums upfront and hold them as investments until claims are paid. The balance sheet is dominated by investments on the asset side and technical provisions on the liability side, unlike a typical trading company."},
        {q:"55. What is reinsurance and why do insurers buy it?", a:"Insurance for insurers. It reduces volatility, protects against large and catastrophe losses, increases capacity to write business, reduces capital requirements and provides expertise. Types: proportional (quota share, surplus) and non-proportional (excess of loss, stop loss)."},
        {q:"56. What is the dividend growth model?", a:"Share price P0 = D1 / (k - g), where D1 is next year's dividend, k the cost of equity and g the constant growth rate. Rearranged, it gives the cost of equity k = D1/P0 + g."},
        {q:"57. What are the assumptions and limitations of CAPM?", a:"Assumes investors are rational, diversified, can borrow and lend at the risk-free rate, single period, no taxes or costs. Limitations: beta is unstable and estimated from past data, the market portfolio is unobservable, and empirical evidence shows other factors (size, value) matter."},
        {q:"58. When is WACC not an appropriate discount rate?", a:"When the project has different risk from the company's existing business, or when it changes the capital structure. Then use a project-specific rate, e.g. a proxy company's beta adjusted for gearing (adjusted present value or project-specific WACC)."},
        {q:"59. What is APV (adjusted present value)?", a:"Value a project as if all-equity financed, then add the present value of financing side-effects such as the tax shield on debt and subtract issue costs. Useful when financing changes gearing."},
        {q:"60. What is the efficient markets hypothesis?", a:"Share prices reflect available information. Weak form: past prices; semi-strong: all public information; strong: all information including private. If markets are efficient, it is hard to consistently beat the market."},
        {q:"61. What are the main methods of valuing a company?", a:"Asset-based (net asset value), earnings-based (P/E multiples), dividend-based (dividend growth model), and cash flow-based (discounted free cash flow). For insurers, embedded value or Solvency II own funds are also used."},
        {q:"62. Why do companies merge or acquire others?", a:"Synergies (cost savings, revenue gains), economies of scale, market power, diversification, acquiring skills or technology, tax benefits and growth. Many fail to deliver value due to overpayment and integration problems."},
        {q:"63. What are the methods of paying for an acquisition?", a:"Cash (from reserves or borrowing) or share exchange. Cash gives certainty to targets but uses liquidity; shares preserve cash but dilute ownership and transfer risk to the target's shareholders."},
        {q:"64. What is the difference between a company's book value and market value?", a:"Book value is the net assets in the accounts, based on historical cost and accounting rules. Market value is the share price times the number of shares, reflecting expected future profits. The ratio is the price-to-book ratio."},
        {q:"65. What is a private equity investment?", a:"Investment in unlisted companies, often through leveraged buyouts, aiming to improve the business and exit through a sale or IPO. Investors accept illiquidity for higher expected returns."},
        {q:"66. What are covenants in a loan agreement?", a:"Restrictions agreed by the borrower to protect lenders, such as maximum gearing, minimum interest cover, limits on dividends or further borrowing. Breach can trigger repayment."},
        {q:"67. What is factoring and invoice discounting?", a:"Selling trade receivables to a third party for immediate cash (factoring), often with collection services, or borrowing against them (invoice discounting). Improves cash flow at a cost."},
        {q:"68. What is overtrading?", a:"Growing sales too quickly without enough working capital, causing liquidity problems even though the business is profitable. Signs include rising receivables, inventory and overdraft."},
        {q:"69. What is the cash conversion cycle?", a:"Inventory days + receivables days - payables days: the time between paying for inputs and receiving cash from customers. Shorter cycles mean less working capital needed."},
        {q:"70. What are the differences between a mutual and a proprietary insurer?", a:"A mutual is owned by its policyholders, with no shareholders; profits are returned through lower premiums or bonuses, and raising capital is harder. A proprietary insurer is owned by shareholders, can raise equity easily, but must balance shareholder returns against customer outcomes."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 4: Applied and scenario questions", items: [
        {q:"71. How would you assess an insurer's financial health from its accounts?", a:"Look at combined ratio trend, reserve development (prior year releases or strengthening), investment return, return on equity, solvency coverage ratio, reinsurance dependence, gearing and cash position, plus notes on reserving and risk."},
        {q:"72. An insurer reports a profit but a combined ratio of 105%. How?", a:"It made an underwriting loss but investment income and possibly reserve releases from prior years more than offset it. This is sustainable only while investment returns are strong."},
        {q:"73. What does a reserve release mean?", a:"Prior years' claims are settling for less than originally reserved, so the excess is released into profit. Persistent large releases may suggest over-reserving; deteriorations suggest under-reserving."},
        {q:"74. How might rising interest rates affect insurers' financial statements?", a:"Bond values fall, reducing assets, but discounted liabilities (under IFRS 17 and Solvency) also fall. Future investment income rises. The net effect depends on asset-liability matching."},
        {q:"75. Why would an insurer issue subordinated debt?", a:"It counts as Tier 2 regulatory capital under Solvency UK, increasing solvency coverage without diluting shareholders, while being cheaper than equity."},
        {q:"76. Should a company use debt or equity to fund expansion?", a:"Consider current gearing, interest cover, cost of each source, cash flow stability, tax position, control issues, market conditions and covenants. Stable cash flows support more debt; volatile businesses should rely more on equity."},
        {q:"77. How would you explain WACC to a manager?", a:"It is the average return the company has to pay its lenders and shareholders. Any new project should earn more than this, otherwise it destroys value."},
        {q:"78. How would you explain the combined ratio to a non-insurance person?", a:"For every pound of premium we earn, how much we spend on claims and costs. Below 100p means we make money from insurance itself."},
        {q:"79. What would cause a company with good profits to run out of cash?", a:"Overtrading, slow customer payments, heavy capital expenditure, high inventory, large debt repayments or tax and dividend payments exceeding operating cash flow."},
        {q:"80. How can a company improve its cash position?", a:"Collect receivables faster, negotiate longer supplier credit, reduce inventory, delay non-essential capital spending, sell or lease back assets, raise new finance, or cut dividends."},
        {q:"81. What is the role of the actuarial function in an insurer under Solvency UK?", a:"Coordinating and validating technical provisions, giving an opinion on underwriting policy and reinsurance arrangements, and contributing to the risk management system."},
        {q:"82. How is profit measured for a life insurer vs a GI insurer?", a:"Life profits emerge slowly over long contracts and depend heavily on reserving assumptions and investment returns (embedded value, CSM release). GI profits depend mainly on short-term underwriting results, claims reserving and investment income."},
        {q:"83. What could a large prior year reserve strengthening signal?", a:"Earlier under-reserving, claims inflation not anticipated, legal or court changes, adverse development in long-tail lines, or weaker reserving discipline. It reduces profit and capital."},
        {q:"84. How do accounting choices affect reported profit?", a:"Depreciation methods, provisioning, revenue recognition, valuation of assets, reserving assumptions and discount rates. Understanding these is essential when comparing companies."},
        {q:"85. What is creative accounting or window dressing?", a:"Using accounting choices to make results look better than reality, e.g. delaying expenses, accelerating revenue, releasing reserves. Auditors, regulators and standards aim to limit it."},
        {q:"86. What are the main stakeholders of an insurer?", a:"Shareholders, policyholders, employees, regulators (PRA and FCA), reinsurers, brokers, creditors, rating agencies and society. Their interests can conflict, e.g. shareholder returns vs policyholder security."},
        {q:"87. Why are credit ratings important to insurers?", a:"Ratings from agencies like S&P and AM Best affect brokers' and clients' willingness to buy, particularly in commercial and reinsurance markets, and the cost of borrowing."},
        {q:"88. How would you evaluate a business proposal for a startup?", a:"Assess market size, competition, cost structure, revenue model, cash flow forecasts, NPV, breakeven, funding needs, risks and management team. Use sensitivity analysis on key assumptions."},
        {q:"89. What is breakeven analysis?", a:"Finding the sales level where total revenue equals total costs: breakeven units = fixed costs / contribution per unit, where contribution = price - variable cost. It shows risk through the margin of safety."},
        {q:"90. What is contribution and why is it useful?", a:"Selling price minus variable cost per unit. It shows how much each sale contributes to covering fixed costs and profit, useful for pricing, product mix and short-term decisions."},
        {q:"91. How do you treat sunk costs in decision making?", a:"Ignore them. They have already been incurred and cannot be recovered, so only future incremental cashflows are relevant."},
        {q:"92. What is the difference between fixed and variable costs?", a:"Fixed costs do not change with output in the short run (rent, salaries). Variable costs vary with output (materials, commission). In insurance, commission is variable, while IT and premises are fixed."},
        {q:"93. What are the methods of raising finance for a small startup?", a:"Founders' own funds, friends and family, bank loans, government grants and schemes, angel investors, venture capital, crowdfunding and invoice finance. Each trades off cost, control and risk."},
        {q:"94. How would you decide between a venture capital offer and a bank loan for a startup?", a:"A loan keeps ownership but requires repayments and security, risky with uncertain revenues. Venture capital gives cash without repayments plus expertise, but costs equity and some control. Early-stage, high-growth firms usually suit equity."},
        {q:"95. What are the risks of high gearing for an insurer?", a:"Reduced capital buffer, higher fixed financing costs, potential rating downgrades, regulatory concerns about solvency, and less flexibility to absorb large losses."},
        {q:"96. What is embedded value?", a:"A measure of the value of a life insurer: net worth plus the present value of future profits from existing business, less the cost of capital. It values in-force business not reflected in standard accounts."},
        {q:"97. How does tax affect business finance decisions?", a:"Interest deductibility favours debt, capital allowances affect investment decisions, dividend taxation affects payout policy, and group structures and locations are influenced by tax rates."},
        {q:"98. Which CB1 topic did you find most interesting?", a:"Pick one and link it to your goals, e.g. reading insurer accounts because it shows how reserving and pricing decisions flow into reported profit."},
        {q:"99. Describe a time you used business finance knowledge.", a:"Use a real example from your startups, e.g. Intellilink or Sanjh Design House: estimating costs, setting prices, deciding how to fund the business or forecasting cash flow. Use aim, method, result, learning."},
        {q:"100. If you could explain one CB1 idea that matters most for an actuary, what would it be?", a:"Capital: how much an insurer needs, what it costs (WACC and cost of equity) and how it is allocated. It links pricing, reinsurance, investment strategy and solvency."}
      ]}
    ]
  },
  cb2: {
    code: "CB2",
    name: "Business Economics",
    note: "100 interview questions ranked by how often they're asked in UK actuarial analyst interviews. Cover each answer and say yours aloud in under 60 seconds. Questions 1–20 should be flawless. Questions 76 to 80, 89 and 95 are the GI differentiators.",
    tiers: [
      { name: "Tier 1: Asked in almost every interview", items: [
        {q:"1. What is adverse selection? Give an insurance example.", a:"Adverse selection arises before a contract is signed when one party has more information than the other. In insurance, higher-risk people are more likely to buy cover at a given price. If the insurer charges an average price, low risks leave and high risks stay, pushing claims and prices up. Insurers counter it with underwriting, risk-based pricing, rating factors and compulsory or group cover."},
        {q:"2. What is moral hazard? Give an insurance example.", a:"Moral hazard arises after a contract is signed when a party changes behaviour because they are protected from the consequences. An insured driver may take less care, or a policyholder may not lock up property. Insurers control it with excesses, co-insurance, no-claims discounts, policy conditions and monitoring such as telematics."},
        {q:"3. What is the difference between adverse selection and moral hazard?", a:"Both come from asymmetric information. Adverse selection is hidden information before the contract (who buys); moral hazard is hidden action after the contract (how they behave). Underwriting tackles adverse selection; excesses and NCDs tackle moral hazard."},
        {q:"4. Explain price elasticity of demand.", a:"The percentage change in quantity demanded divided by the percentage change in price. If its absolute value exceeds 1, demand is elastic and a price rise reduces revenue; below 1 it is inelastic and a price rise increases revenue. Determinants: availability of substitutes, proportion of income, necessity, time period and habit."},
        {q:"5. Is demand for car insurance elastic or inelastic?", a:"At the market level it is inelastic because cover is compulsory. For an individual insurer it is highly elastic, especially on price comparison websites where substitutes are one click away. That is why small price changes strongly affect conversion and retention, and why insurers model price elasticity in pricing."},
        {q:"6. What are the main market structures?", a:"Perfect competition (many firms, identical products, price takers, normal profit long run), monopolistic competition (many firms, differentiated products), oligopoly (few large interdependent firms) and monopoly (single seller, price maker, barriers to entry). Insurance markets are often close to monopolistic competition or oligopoly in specialist lines."},
        {q:"7. What is inflation and what causes it?", a:"A sustained rise in the general price level. Causes: demand-pull (aggregate demand exceeding supply), cost-push (rising wages, energy or import costs) and monetary (excess money growth). Expectations can make inflation self-reinforcing through wage demands and price setting."},
        {q:"8. How does the Bank of England control inflation?", a:"Through monetary policy: setting Bank Rate to meet the 2% CPI target set by the government. Higher rates raise borrowing costs, reduce spending and investment, strengthen sterling and dampen inflation. It also uses quantitative easing and tightening, and forward guidance to influence expectations."},
        {q:"9. What is the difference between monetary and fiscal policy?", a:"Monetary policy is run by the central bank through interest rates and the money supply. Fiscal policy is run by the government through taxation and spending. Fiscal policy can target specific sectors but has longer implementation lags and political constraints; monetary policy is quicker but blunter."},
        {q:"10. How do higher interest rates affect an insurance company?", a:"Positive: higher investment yields and lower discounted value of liabilities. Negative: falls in the market value of existing bonds, possible economic slowdown reducing demand, and higher claims inflation often accompanies rising rates. Life annuity books benefit as annuity prices fall."},
        {q:"11. How does inflation affect insurers?", a:"Claims inflation increases repair, replacement, medical and legal costs, especially for long-tail lines where claims settle years later. If not anticipated in premiums and reserves, it erodes profits. It also affects expenses and investment values. Recent UK motor claims inflation is a strong example."},
        {q:"12. What is GDP and how is it measured?", a:"Gross domestic product is the total value of final goods and services produced in a country in a period. Three approaches give the same answer in principle: output (value added), income (wages, profits, rents) and expenditure (C + I + G + X - M). Real GDP adjusts for inflation."},
        {q:"13. What is a recession and how might it affect insurance?", a:"Typically two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP. Effects: lower demand for discretionary cover, more lapses, higher fraud, possible rise in some claims (e.g. credit, unemployment-related), lower investment returns and pressure on premiums."},
        {q:"14. Explain supply and demand and how equilibrium is reached.", a:"Demand falls as price rises; supply rises as price rises. Equilibrium is where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. Excess demand pushes prices up; excess supply pushes them down. Shifts in demand or supply change the equilibrium price and quantity."},
        {q:"15. What is the underwriting cycle and how does economics explain it?", a:"GI markets swing between soft markets (low prices, easy capacity, falling profits) and hard markets (high prices, restricted capacity). Drivers: competition for market share, entry of new capital when profits are high, catastrophes or reserve deteriorations removing capital, and interest rate changes affecting investment income. It is a supply-side cycle."},
        {q:"16. What is market failure?", a:"When free markets fail to allocate resources efficiently. Causes: externalities, public goods, information asymmetry, monopoly power and missing markets. Insurance examples: adverse selection leading to uninsurable risks (flood), requiring schemes like Flood Re."},
        {q:"17. What are externalities?", a:"Costs or benefits to third parties not reflected in prices. Negative example: pollution or uninsured drivers imposing costs on others. Positive example: vaccination. Governments respond with taxes, subsidies, regulation or compulsory insurance."},
        {q:"18. What is the role of the FCA and PRA?", a:"The PRA (part of the Bank of England) is the prudential regulator, ensuring insurers and banks are financially sound (Solvency UK). The FCA regulates conduct, protecting consumers and market integrity, e.g. Consumer Duty and the ban on price walking."},
        {q:"19. Why was price walking banned in UK insurance?", a:"Insurers charged loyal renewing customers more than new customers for the same risk, exploiting inertia. The FCA's 2022 rules require renewal prices to be no higher than the equivalent new business price. Economically it was a form of price discrimination exploiting inelastic existing customers."},
        {q:"20. Which CB2 topic is most relevant to an actuary in GI?", a:"Asymmetric information (adverse selection and moral hazard), price elasticity, market structure and competition, and the macro effects of inflation and interest rates on claims and investments. These directly shape pricing, underwriting and reserving."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 2: Very common follow-ups", items: [
        {q:"21. What is price discrimination?", a:"Charging different prices to different customers for the same product for reasons unrelated to cost. Requires market power, separable markets and different elasticities. In insurance, risk-based pricing is not discrimination as it reflects cost, but pricing on elasticity (e.g. loyalty penalties) is, and is now restricted."},
        {q:"22. What is income elasticity of demand?", a:"Percentage change in quantity demanded divided by percentage change in income. Positive for normal goods; above 1 for luxury goods; negative for inferior goods. Optional insurance like pet or gadget cover tends to be income elastic."},
        {q:"23. What is cross elasticity of demand?", a:"Percentage change in demand for one good divided by percentage change in price of another. Positive for substitutes (two insurers' products), negative for complements (cars and car insurance)."},
        {q:"24. What determines price elasticity of supply?", a:"Spare capacity, ease of switching production, stock levels, time period and availability of inputs. In insurance, supply depends on available capital; after a catastrophe, capital is scarce and supply is inelastic in the short run."},
        {q:"25. What is consumer surplus and producer surplus?", a:"Consumer surplus is the difference between what consumers are willing to pay and what they pay. Producer surplus is the difference between the price received and the minimum the producer would accept. Their sum is total welfare, maximised at competitive equilibrium."},
        {q:"26. What is the law of diminishing marginal returns?", a:"In the short run, adding more of a variable input to a fixed input eventually causes the extra output from each additional unit to fall. It explains why short-run marginal cost curves eventually rise."},
        {q:"27. Explain economies of scale.", a:"Falling long-run average cost as output grows. Sources: technical (larger systems), purchasing, managerial specialisation, financial (cheaper capital) and risk-bearing (diversification). Insurers gain from spreading fixed IT and pricing costs and from more predictable claims through the law of large numbers."},
        {q:"28. What are diseconomies of scale?", a:"Rising average cost when a firm becomes too large, due to coordination problems, communication failures, bureaucracy and low motivation."},
        {q:"29. Short run vs long run in economics?", a:"In the short run at least one factor of production is fixed. In the long run all factors are variable and firms can enter or leave the market."},
        {q:"30. What is the profit-maximising condition?", a:"Produce where marginal revenue equals marginal cost, provided price covers average variable cost in the short run and average total cost in the long run."},
        {q:"31. What are barriers to entry?", a:"Factors preventing new firms entering a market: economies of scale, high capital requirements, regulation and licensing, brand loyalty, access to distribution and data. In insurance, capital requirements, regulation and pricing data are key barriers."},
        {q:"32. Explain oligopoly and game theory.", a:"In an oligopoly a few firms are interdependent, so each must consider rivals' reactions. Game theory models this, e.g. the prisoner's dilemma shows why firms may compete prices down even if collusion would benefit both. Outcomes include price rigidity (kinked demand curve) and non-price competition."},
        {q:"33. What is a Nash equilibrium?", a:"A situation where each player's strategy is the best response to the others' strategies, so no one has an incentive to change unilaterally."},
        {q:"34. What is the principal-agent problem?", a:"When an agent (manager, broker) acts on behalf of a principal (shareholder, client) but has different objectives and more information. Solutions: performance-related pay, monitoring, contracts aligning incentives. In insurance, brokers' commission incentives are an example."},
        {q:"35. What is signalling and screening?", a:"Signalling: the informed party reveals information credibly, e.g. a driver installing a telematics box. Screening: the uninformed party designs choices to make others reveal information, e.g. offering policies with different excesses so low risks choose high excesses."},
        {q:"36. What is behavioural economics?", a:"Studies how people deviate from rational choices due to biases: loss aversion, present bias, overconfidence, framing, anchoring and inertia. In insurance it explains under-insurance, preference for low excesses, and renewal inertia."},
        {q:"37. What is loss aversion and how does it relate to insurance?", a:"People feel losses more strongly than equivalent gains. It explains why people buy insurance and extended warranties even at poor value, and prefer low excesses."},
        {q:"38. What is unemployment and what are its types?", a:"People willing and able to work without a job. Types: frictional (between jobs), structural (mismatch of skills or location), cyclical (demand deficient, in recessions) and seasonal."},
        {q:"39. What is the Phillips curve?", a:"An inverse short-run relationship between unemployment and inflation. In the long run it is believed to be vertical at the natural rate of unemployment, as expectations adjust, so there is no lasting trade-off."},
        {q:"40. What is aggregate demand and aggregate supply?", a:"AD = C + I + G + (X - M), total spending at each price level. AS is total output firms supply. Short-run AS slopes upward; long-run AS is vertical at potential output. Shifts explain inflation and output changes."},
        {q:"41. What is the multiplier?", a:"An initial increase in spending causes a larger final increase in national income because one person's spending is another's income. Multiplier = 1/(1 - MPC), reduced by taxes, savings and imports."},
        {q:"42. What is quantitative easing?", a:"The central bank creates money to buy assets, mainly government bonds, lowering long-term yields and encouraging spending when Bank Rate is near zero. For insurers it reduced bond yields, raising liability values and lowering investment income."},
        {q:"43. What are exchange rates and what affects them?", a:"The price of one currency in another. Affected by interest rate differentials, inflation, trade balance, capital flows, expectations and political stability. A weaker pound raises import costs, e.g. car parts, feeding into claims inflation."},
        {q:"44. What is the balance of payments?", a:"A record of a country's transactions with the rest of the world: the current account (trade, income, transfers), the capital account and the financial account. It must balance overall."},
        {q:"45. What is the business cycle?", a:"Fluctuations of real GDP around its trend: expansion, peak, contraction (recession) and trough. Drivers include changes in demand, investment, credit, confidence and shocks."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 3: Asked by more technical interviewers", items: [
        {q:"46. What is utility and diminishing marginal utility?", a:"Utility is satisfaction from consumption. Diminishing marginal utility means each additional unit gives less extra satisfaction. A risk-averse person has a concave utility function, which is why they pay more than expected loss for insurance."},
        {q:"47. Explain indifference curves and budget constraints.", a:"Indifference curves show combinations of goods giving equal utility; they slope down and are convex. The budget line shows affordable combinations. The consumer's optimum is where the highest indifference curve touches the budget line, where the marginal rate of substitution equals the price ratio."},
        {q:"48. What are income and substitution effects?", a:"When a price falls, the substitution effect makes the good relatively cheaper so people buy more. The income effect increases real income, increasing demand for normal goods and reducing it for inferior goods. Giffen goods are where a strong inverse income effect outweighs substitution."},
        {q:"49. What are normal, inferior and Giffen goods?", a:"Normal goods: demand rises with income. Inferior goods: demand falls with income (cheap substitutes). Giffen goods: demand rises when price rises, a rare case of a strongly inferior staple."},
        {q:"50. What is a contestable market?", a:"A market with low barriers to entry and exit, so even a monopolist must keep prices close to competitive levels due to the threat of entry."},
        {q:"51. What is the kinked demand curve?", a:"In oligopoly, firms expect rivals to match price cuts but not price rises, so demand is elastic above the current price and inelastic below it. This explains price rigidity."},
        {q:"52. What is allocative and productive efficiency?", a:"Allocative efficiency: price equals marginal cost, producing what society values most. Productive efficiency: producing at minimum average cost. Perfect competition achieves both in the long run; monopoly typically achieves neither."},
        {q:"53. What is deadweight loss?", a:"The loss of total welfare when output is below the efficient level, e.g. from monopoly pricing or taxes."},
        {q:"54. What are public goods?", a:"Goods that are non-rival and non-excludable, such as defence or street lighting. Free-rider problems mean markets under-provide them, so government provides them."},
        {q:"55. What is a merit good?", a:"A good under-consumed because people underestimate its benefits, such as education or pensions. Government encourages consumption, e.g. auto-enrolment into pensions."},
        {q:"56. What are the components of money supply and how is money created?", a:"Narrow money (notes and coins, reserves) and broad money (including bank deposits). Most money is created by commercial banks lending, constrained by demand for loans, regulation and central bank rates."},
        {q:"57. What is the transmission mechanism of monetary policy?", a:"Bank Rate changes affect market interest rates, asset prices, the exchange rate and expectations, which change spending and investment, then aggregate demand, then output and inflation, with lags of up to two years."},
        {q:"58. What are the limitations of fiscal policy?", a:"Time lags, crowding out of private investment, political pressures, rising government debt and uncertainty about the size of the multiplier."},
        {q:"59. What is crowding out?", a:"Higher government borrowing raises interest rates and reduces private investment, partly offsetting the effect of fiscal expansion."},
        {q:"60. What is the natural rate of unemployment?", a:"The unemployment rate when the labour market is in equilibrium, consisting of frictional and structural unemployment. Pushing unemployment below it causes accelerating inflation."},
        {q:"61. What is stagflation?", a:"High inflation combined with stagnant growth and high unemployment, typically caused by supply shocks such as energy price rises. It is hard to fight because tightening worsens unemployment."},
        {q:"62. What is deflation and why is it harmful?", a:"A sustained fall in the price level. People delay spending, debt burdens rise in real terms and monetary policy is constrained by the zero lower bound."},
        {q:"63. What is the difference between CPI and RPI?", a:"Both measure inflation. CPI is the UK's official target measure using a geometric mean formula. RPI uses an arithmetic mean and includes housing costs like mortgage interest, so it is usually higher. Some pensions and index-linked gilts historically used RPI."},
        {q:"64. Why are banks and financial institutions regulated?", a:"Because of systemic risk, information asymmetry, consumer protection and the costs of failure to the wider economy, shown in the 2008 crisis."},
        {q:"65. What caused the 2008 financial crisis?", a:"Excessive lending, sub-prime mortgages, securitisation that hid risk, high leverage, reliance on short-term funding and weak regulation. Falling house prices triggered losses, banks failed and credit dried up, causing a global recession."},
        {q:"66. What is the role of a central bank?", a:"Setting monetary policy, issuing currency, acting as lender of last resort, regulating and supervising banks and maintaining financial stability."},
        {q:"67. Fixed vs floating exchange rates?", a:"Floating rates are set by markets and act as automatic stabilisers but are volatile. Fixed rates give certainty but require reserves and sacrifice independent monetary policy."},
        {q:"68. What is globalisation and its effect on insurance?", a:"Increasing integration of markets. Insurers face more competition, can diversify risks internationally and access global reinsurance and capital markets, but also face contagion and regulatory complexity."},
        {q:"69. What is comparative advantage?", a:"A country should specialise in goods it can produce at lower opportunity cost than others, and trade, making all parties better off."},
        {q:"70. What is opportunity cost?", a:"The value of the next best alternative given up when making a choice. For an insurer, capital held against one line of business cannot support another, so capital allocation decisions compare returns across lines."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 4: Applied and scenario questions", items: [
        {q:"71. What is the difference between microeconomics and macroeconomics?", a:"Microeconomics studies individual consumers, firms and markets (prices, elasticity, competition). Macroeconomics studies the whole economy (GDP, inflation, unemployment, interest rates, policy)."},
        {q:"72. What is the accelerator principle?", a:"Investment depends on the rate of change of output, not its level. Small changes in demand can cause large swings in investment, amplifying the business cycle."},
        {q:"73. What is the quantity theory of money?", a:"MV = PT: money supply times velocity equals price level times transactions. If velocity and output are stable, increases in the money supply lead to proportional inflation."},
        {q:"74. What is the liquidity trap?", a:"When interest rates are near zero and people hoard cash, so further monetary easing fails to stimulate spending. Fiscal policy or QE are then needed."},
        {q:"75. What is the difference between nominal and real values?", a:"Nominal values are in current prices; real values adjust for inflation to show changes in actual volume or purchasing power."},
        {q:"76. A competitor cuts motor premiums by 10%. What do you do?", a:"Assess whether the cut is sustainable or loss-making, our elasticity and retention risk, which segments are affected, and our own loss ratios. Avoid matching blindly; protect profitable segments, consider non-price competition and monitor whether the market is softening."},
        {q:"77. How would claims inflation affect your pricing?", a:"Project frequency and severity trends forward to the policy period, using indices for repair costs, wages and parts. Underestimating inflation causes underpricing. Monitor actual versus expected regularly."},
        {q:"78. Why might an insurer leave a market?", a:"Persistent losses, inability to compete on price, insufficient scale, high capital requirements, regulatory burden or a strategic shift to more profitable lines."},
        {q:"79. How might a cost of living crisis affect GI?", a:"More policy cancellations and lower cover levels, more price shopping, higher fraud risk, higher claims inflation and increased regulatory focus on fair value under Consumer Duty."},
        {q:"80. What is Consumer Duty and why does it matter?", a:"FCA rules from 2023 requiring firms to deliver good outcomes for retail customers: fair value, clear communications, suitable products and support. It affects pricing, product design and claims handling."},
        {q:"81. How does economics inform pricing beyond pure risk cost?", a:"Demand elasticity, competitor prices, market cycle, regulatory limits on price optimisation, customer lifetime value and expenses all influence the final price above the technical premium."},
        {q:"82. Why does compulsory insurance exist?", a:"To deal with externalities (uninsured drivers harming others) and adverse selection. Motor third-party and employers' liability are compulsory in the UK."},
        {q:"83. What is Flood Re and why was it created?", a:"A UK reinsurance scheme making flood cover affordable for high-risk homes. Without it adverse selection and risk-based pricing would make cover unaffordable, a market failure."},
        {q:"84. How would a rise in Bank Rate affect demand for insurance?", a:"Mortgage and borrowing costs rise, reducing disposable income, so demand for optional cover falls and customers shop around more. Compulsory cover is less affected."},
        {q:"85. Explain the impact of Brexit on UK insurers.", a:"Loss of EU passporting, need for EU subsidiaries, regulatory divergence (Solvency UK), currency effects on claims costs and changes in labour supply."},
        {q:"86. How do catastrophes affect the reinsurance market?", a:"Large losses reduce capital, so reinsurance supply falls and prices rise (a hard market). Eventually high prices attract new capital, including insurance-linked securities, and prices soften."},
        {q:"87. What role do price comparison websites play?", a:"They increase price transparency, make demand highly elastic, intensify competition and lower margins, but also lower acquisition costs. They favour insurers with sophisticated pricing."},
        {q:"88. How might technology change insurance markets?", a:"Telematics and data reduce information asymmetry, enable more precise pricing and new products, lower costs through automation and lower barriers for insurtech entrants."},
        {q:"89. Why might an insurer use a high excess option?", a:"It reduces moral hazard, lowers claims handling costs from small claims and screens risks, as lower risks choose higher excesses for lower premiums."},
        {q:"90. How would you explain adverse selection to a non-actuary?", a:"If you charge everyone the same price, the people who know they are risky are the ones who most want to buy, so you end up with a riskier group than you expected."},
        {q:"91. What is the link between CB2 and actuarial work?", a:"It explains customer behaviour, competition, regulation and the macroeconomy, which drive pricing, claims trends, investment returns and strategy."},
        {q:"92. What economic indicators would you watch as a GI actuary?", a:"CPI and wage inflation, specific claims indices (repair, parts, construction costs), Bank Rate and gilt yields, GDP growth, unemployment and exchange rates."},
        {q:"93. What is the economic rationale for insurance?", a:"Risk-averse individuals gain utility by paying a certain premium to avoid uncertain large losses. Insurers pool many independent risks, so by the law of large numbers their average claims are predictable."},
        {q:"94. Why are some risks uninsurable?", a:"Lack of independence (systemic or catastrophe risks), moral hazard, adverse selection, unmeasurable probabilities, or losses too large relative to capital, e.g. pandemic business interruption or war."},
        {q:"95. How would you assess market attractiveness for a new insurance product?", a:"Market size and growth, competition and barriers to entry, expected loss ratios, elasticity, distribution, regulation, capital requirements and fit with the company's strategy."},
        {q:"96. What is the impact of an ageing population?", a:"Higher demand for pensions, annuities, health and long-term care, pressure on public finances, changing motor risk profiles and labour shortages that may raise claims costs."},
        {q:"97. Which CB2 topic did you find most interesting?", a:"Choose one and link it to work, e.g. asymmetric information because it explains why underwriting, rating factors and excesses exist."},
        {q:"98. Describe a time you applied economic thinking.", a:"Use a real example from your startups or retail work: e.g. setting prices at Sanjh Design House considering customer willingness to pay, competitors and costs."},
        {q:"99. How do interest rates influence the underwriting cycle?", a:"High investment income allows insurers to accept higher combined ratios (cash flow underwriting), softening prices. Low rates force underwriting discipline and harder pricing."},
        {q:"100. If you could explain one CB2 idea that matters most in GI, what would it be?", a:"Asymmetric information, because adverse selection and moral hazard explain almost every feature of an insurance product: underwriting questions, rating factors, excesses and no-claims discounts."}
      ]}
    ]
  },
  cm1: {
    code: "CM1",
    name: "Actuarial Mathematics",
    note: "100 interview questions ranked by how often they're asked in UK actuarial analyst interviews. Cover each answer and say yours aloud in under 60 seconds. Questions 1–20 should be flawless. For GI roles, questions 76 to 80, 83 and 89 are the differentiators.",
    tiers: [
      { name: "Tier 1: Asked in almost every technical interview", items: [
        {q:"1. What is the time value of money?", a:"Money today is worth more than the same amount in the future because it can be invested to earn interest, and because of inflation and uncertainty. All actuarial valuation rests on this idea: future cashflows must be discounted to compare them with present ones."},
        {q:"2. What is the difference between simple and compound interest?", a:"Simple interest is earned only on the original principal, so it grows linearly: P(1 + in). Compound interest is earned on accumulated interest too, so it grows exponentially: P(1 + i)^n. Compound interest is used for almost all actuarial work; simple interest is used for some short-term money market instruments."},
        {q:"3. Explain effective, nominal and force of interest.", a:"The effective rate i is the actual interest earned over a year per unit invested. A nominal rate i(p) is quoted per annum but credited p times a year at i(p)/p each period, so (1 + i(p)/p)^p = 1 + i. The force of interest delta is the continuous limit: e^delta = 1 + i, so delta = ln(1 + i)."},
        {q:"4. What is the difference between a discount rate and an interest rate?", a:"Interest i is paid at the end of the period on the amount at the start. Discount d is deducted at the start of the period on the amount at the end. They are linked by d = i/(1 + i) = iv, where v = 1/(1 + i) is the discount factor."},
        {q:"5. What is net present value (NPV)?", a:"The sum of all future cashflows discounted to today at a chosen rate, minus the initial outlay. A positive NPV means the project earns more than the required rate of return and adds value; a negative NPV means it destroys value."},
        {q:"6. What is the internal rate of return (IRR)?", a:"The interest rate at which the NPV of a project's cashflows is zero. Accept a project if its IRR exceeds the cost of capital. It is intuitive but can be misleading: there may be multiple IRRs or none when cashflows change sign more than once, and it ignores project scale."},
        {q:"7. NPV or IRR: which is better for choosing between projects?", a:"NPV is generally better. It measures absolute value created, works with any cashflow pattern and uses a realistic reinvestment rate. IRR can rank mutually exclusive projects wrongly because it ignores size and assumes reinvestment at the IRR itself. Use IRR as a supporting measure."},
        {q:"8. What is an annuity and how do you value it?", a:"A series of regular payments. The present value of an annuity of 1 paid in arrears for n years is a_n = (1 - v^n)/i; paid in advance it is a-double-dot_n = (1 - v^n)/d. Annuities are used to value loans, bonds and pensions."},
        {q:"9. What is duration and why does it matter?", a:"Macaulay (discounted mean) term is the weighted average time to receive cashflows, weighted by present value. Modified duration (effective duration) measures the percentage change in price for a small change in yield: -P'(i)/P(i). It is the key measure of interest rate risk for bonds and liabilities."},
        {q:"10. What is convexity?", a:"The second-order measure of how price responds to yield changes: P''(i)/P(i). Duration gives a straight-line approximation; convexity captures the curvature. Higher convexity is valuable because prices rise more when yields fall and fall less when yields rise."},
        {q:"11. What is Redington immunisation?", a:"A strategy to protect a fund against small changes in interest rates. Three conditions: present value of assets equals present value of liabilities; their durations (volatilities) are equal; and the convexity of assets exceeds that of liabilities. Then a small rate change produces a surplus, not a deficit."},
        {q:"12. What are the limitations of Redington immunisation?", a:"It only protects against small, parallel shifts in a flat yield curve. It requires frequent rebalancing, which costs money. Suitable long-dated assets may not exist. It ignores credit and default risk, and liability cashflows may be uncertain."},
        {q:"13. Explain the term structure of interest rates.", a:"The relationship between interest rates and term to maturity, shown by the yield curve. Spot rates are yields on zero-coupon bonds for each term; forward rates are rates agreed now for future periods; par yields are coupon rates giving bonds priced at par."},
        {q:"14. Why does the yield curve usually slope upwards?", a:"Three theories: expectations theory (markets expect rates to rise), liquidity preference (investors demand a premium for tying up money longer) and market segmentation (different investors favour different terms, so supply and demand set each part of the curve). An inverted curve often signals expected falls in rates or recession."},
        {q:"15. How do you value a fixed-interest bond?", a:"Price = present value of coupons + present value of redemption: P = D a_n + R v^n at the investor's required yield, adjusting for tax where relevant. A bond trades at a premium if its coupon rate exceeds the market yield and at a discount if below."},
        {q:"16. What is the relationship between bond prices and yields?", a:"They move inversely. If yields rise, future fixed cashflows are discounted more heavily so the price falls. Long-dated and low-coupon bonds have higher duration and so are more sensitive to yield changes."},
        {q:"17. Describe the main asset classes and their characteristics.", a:"Government bonds: low risk, fixed income, good for matching. Corporate bonds: higher yield for credit risk. Equities: real returns, high volatility, rising dividends, residual claim. Property: real returns, rental income, illiquid, large indivisible units, high costs. Cash: low risk, low return, liquid. Index-linked bonds: protect against inflation."},
        {q:"18. How would you match assets to liabilities for an insurer?", a:"Match by currency, term and nature. Short-tail GI liabilities suit cash and short bonds. Long-tail liabilities suit longer bonds. Real (inflation-linked) liabilities suit index-linked bonds or equities. Matching reduces risk; deliberate mismatching can increase expected returns at the cost of extra risk."},
        {q:"19. What is a loan schedule and how do you split a repayment into capital and interest?", a:"For a level-repayment loan, the outstanding balance at any time is the present value of remaining payments (prospective) or the original loan accumulated minus payments accumulated (retrospective). Each payment's interest = rate x outstanding balance at the start of the period; the rest repays capital. Early payments are mostly interest."},
        {q:"20. Which CM1 topic is most relevant to general insurance?", a:"Discounting cashflows, valuing assets, duration and asset-liability matching. GI reserves are increasingly discounted (IFRS 17 and Solvency II), investment income affects pricing, and insurers must match assets to claim payout patterns. Mention CM1 also underpins reserving cashflow projections."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 2: Very common follow-ups", items: [
        {q:"21. What is the equation of value?", a:"An equation setting the present value of incoming cashflows equal to the present value of outgoing cashflows at a given rate. It is used to find the yield on a transaction, the fair price of an asset or the required level of payments."},
        {q:"22. What is the discounted payback period?", a:"The time taken for the accumulated discounted net cashflows to become positive. It shows how quickly a project recovers its cost allowing for interest, but it ignores cashflows after that point, so it should not be used alone."},
        {q:"23. What is the effect of inflation on investment returns?", a:"Real return is roughly nominal return minus inflation; exactly, (1 + i') = (1 + i)/(1 + j). Inflation erodes fixed cashflows such as conventional bonds, which is why real assets or index-linked bonds are used for inflation-linked liabilities."},
        {q:"24. Money-weighted vs time-weighted rate of return?", a:"MWRR is the IRR of the fund's cashflows; it depends on the timing and size of contributions, so it reflects both manager skill and cashflow luck. TWRR chains returns between cashflows, removing the effect of new money; it is the fair measure of fund manager performance. LIRR is a linked approximation."},
        {q:"25. What is the difference between spot and forward rates?", a:"A spot rate y_t is the yield on a zero-coupon bond maturing at t, applying from now to t. A forward rate f(t, r) is an interest rate agreed now for borrowing between future times t and t + r. They are linked by (1 + y_t+r)^(t+r) = (1 + y_t)^t (1 + f)^r."},
        {q:"26. What is a par yield?", a:"The coupon rate that makes a bond's price equal to its redemption value, given the current spot curve. Par yields lie between spot rates and are used to set coupons on new bond issues."},
        {q:"27. What is a forward contract and how is it priced?", a:"An agreement to buy or sell an asset at a fixed future time and price. Priced by no-arbitrage: for an asset with no income, K = S0 e^(delta T). With known income I, K = (S0 - I) e^(delta T). With a dividend yield D, K = S0 e^((delta - D)T)."},
        {q:"28. What is the no-arbitrage principle?", a:"In an efficient market there is no way to make a risk-free profit with no initial investment. If two portfolios give identical future payoffs, they must cost the same today. This is the foundation of forward and derivative pricing."},
        {q:"29. What are the main types of derivatives?", a:"Forwards and futures (obligation to trade at a set price; futures are standardised and exchange-traded with margins), options (right but not obligation: calls and puts), and swaps (exchanging cashflows such as fixed for floating interest). Uses: hedging, speculation and arbitrage."},
        {q:"30. What is an interest rate swap?", a:"An agreement where one party pays a fixed rate and the other pays a floating rate on a notional principal. Used to convert floating debt to fixed or vice versa, or to manage asset-liability duration mismatches. Risks are market risk and counterparty credit risk."},
        {q:"31. What is a life table and what is l_x?", a:"A table showing the expected number of survivors l_x at each age from a starting cohort, with d_x = l_x - l_x+1 deaths between ages. Survival and death probabilities follow: p_x = l_x+1/l_x and q_x = d_x/l_x."},
        {q:"32. What is an assurance and how is it valued?", a:"A contract paying a benefit on death (whole life or term) or on survival to a date (pure endowment), or either (endowment). Present value = expected discounted benefit, e.g. whole life A_x = sum of v^(k+1) k|q_x. Values come from mortality and interest assumptions."},
        {q:"33. What is a life annuity?", a:"Payments made while a life survives. The expected present value is a sum of v^k k_p_x. Key relationship: A_x = 1 - d a-double-dot_x, linking assurances and annuities."},
        {q:"34. How is a premium calculated using the equivalence principle?", a:"Set the expected present value of premiums equal to the expected present value of benefits plus expenses. For a level annual premium P on a whole life policy: P a-double-dot_x = S A_x + expenses. It means the insurer expects to break even on the basis used."},
        {q:"35. What is a reserve and why is it needed?", a:"The amount the insurer must hold to meet future obligations under a policy. For level-premium life policies, early premiums exceed the cost of cover and later ones fall short, so a reserve builds up. Without it, the insurer could not pay future claims."},
        {q:"36. Prospective vs retrospective reserves?", a:"Prospective reserve = EPV of future benefits and expenses - EPV of future premiums. Retrospective reserve = accumulated value of past premiums - accumulated cost of past benefits and expenses. They are equal if the same basis is used for both and for the premium basis."},
        {q:"37. What is Thiele's differential equation?", a:"The continuous-time equation describing how a reserve changes: dV/dt = delta V + P - mu (S - V). The reserve grows with interest and premium and falls by the expected cost of death, mu times the sum at risk (S - V)."},
        {q:"38. What is the death strain at risk?", a:"Sum assured (plus any expenses on death) minus the reserve. It is the extra cost to the insurer if the life dies. Expected death strain = q x DSAR; actual death strain = the DSAR for actual deaths. Mortality profit = expected - actual death strain."},
        {q:"39. How do you allow for expenses in premiums?", a:"Include initial expenses (often a fixed amount plus a percentage of first premium), renewal expenses (often a percentage of each premium or fixed per policy, sometimes inflating), and claim expenses. Add their EPV to the benefits side in the equation of value."},
        {q:"40. What is the difference between gross and net premium reserves?", a:"Net premium reserves use a net premium (benefits only) and ignore expenses explicitly; they are conservative and simple. Gross premium reserves use the actual office premium and explicitly allow for future expenses, giving a more realistic valuation."},
        {q:"41. What are joint life and last survivor functions?", a:"A joint life status fails on the first death; a last survivor status fails on the second death. a_xy is an annuity payable while both live; a_xy-bar (last survivor) = a_x + a_y - a_xy. Used for pensions to couples and second-death policies."},
        {q:"42. What is a multiple decrement table?", a:"A table modelling exits from a population for more than one cause, such as death and withdrawal. It gives probabilities of leaving by each cause in the presence of others (dependent rates). Used in pension schemes and lapse-sensitive products."},
        {q:"43. What is a unit-linked contract and how is it valued?", a:"The policyholder's premium buys units in a fund, whose value fluctuates; the insurer deducts charges. The insurer projects two funds: the unit fund (policyholder money) and the non-unit fund (charges minus expenses and extra benefits). Profit testing finds the profit signature and checks for negative cashflows needing reserves (zeroisation)."},
        {q:"44. What is profit testing?", a:"Projecting the cashflows of a policy year by year (premiums, expenses, benefits, interest, reserves) to find the profit vector and profit signature, then discounting at the risk discount rate to give NPV, profit margin, IRR and discounted payback period. Used to set premiums and test assumptions."},
        {q:"45. Explain stochastic interest rate models.", a:"Interest rates are treated as random rather than fixed. In the simplest model, annual returns are independent with mean j and variance s^2; the accumulation is lognormal if 1 + i_t is lognormal. This gives the distribution of fund values, not just a single estimate, which helps quantify investment risk."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 3: Asked by technical or pricing-heavy interviewers", items: [
        {q:"46. What is the formula linking i, d, v and delta?", a:"v = 1/(1 + i), d = 1 - v = iv, delta = ln(1 + i), and e^(-delta) = v. Also i > i(p) > delta > d(p) > d for p > 1."},
        {q:"47. What is an increasing annuity?", a:"Payments of 1, 2, 3 ... n. Present value (Ia)_n = (a-double-dot_n - n v^n)/i. Used to value cashflows that grow linearly, such as increasing benefits or claims."},
        {q:"48. How do you value a perpetuity?", a:"A perpetuity in arrears has PV 1/i; in advance 1/d. A growing perpetuity with growth g and payments starting at 1 next year has PV 1/(i - g), the basis of the dividend discount model for equities."},
        {q:"49. How do you value an equity?", a:"Present value of expected future dividends, often with constant growth: P = D/(i - g). Also relative measures such as P/E and dividend yield. Equity values are highly sensitive to the assumed growth and discount rate."},
        {q:"50. What is a running yield and a gross redemption yield?", a:"Running yield = annual coupon / current price, ignoring redemption gains. Gross redemption yield is the IRR of all coupons and the redemption payment, before tax. The GRY is the standard bond yield measure."},
        {q:"51. How does tax affect bond valuation?", a:"Income tax at t1 reduces each coupon to D(1 - t1). Capital gains tax at t2 applies to any gain (R - P) on redemption. Test for a capital gain by comparing (1 - t1)g with i(p): if i(p) > (1 - t1)g there is a gain."},
        {q:"52. What is the optional redemption date rule?", a:"If the borrower can choose when to redeem, an investor assumes the worst case. If the bond is bought at a premium (coupon above yield), assume the earliest date; at a discount, assume the latest date. This gives the minimum yield for a given price."},
        {q:"53. What is the difference between a futures and forward contract?", a:"Futures are standardised, exchange-traded, marked to market daily with margin through a clearing house, so credit risk is low. Forwards are private over-the-counter agreements, customisable but with counterparty risk and settlement at maturity."},
        {q:"54. What is basis risk?", a:"The risk that the hedging instrument does not move exactly in line with the asset being hedged, e.g. hedging a corporate bond portfolio with government bond futures. The basis is spot price minus futures price."},
        {q:"55. What is a call option and a put option?", a:"A call gives the right to buy an asset at the strike price; a put gives the right to sell. The buyer pays a premium. Calls profit if prices rise above strike; puts if prices fall below strike. Options are used for hedging and leveraged speculation."},
        {q:"56. What is a credit spread?", a:"The extra yield on a corporate bond over a government bond of similar term, compensating for default risk, lower liquidity and sometimes tax. Spreads widen in recessions when default risk rises."},
        {q:"57. What are the characteristics of property as an investment?", a:"Provides rental income that grows with inflation over the long term and potential capital gains. Drawbacks: large unit size, illiquid, high transaction and management costs, valuation is subjective, voids and maintenance risks. It is a real asset suited to long-term real liabilities."},
        {q:"58. What is the difference between conventional and index-linked bonds?", a:"Conventional bonds pay fixed nominal coupons and redemption. Index-linked bonds scale coupons and redemption by an inflation index (with an indexation lag), giving a real return. Index-linked bonds suit inflation-linked liabilities such as indexed pensions or some GI claims."},
        {q:"59. What is the effective duration of a set of liabilities?", a:"The sensitivity of the present value of liabilities to changes in interest rates. Long-tail GI lines like liability insurance have longer durations than short-tail property lines, so their value falls more when rates rise."},
        {q:"60. How do you calculate the Macaulay duration?", a:"Sum of t x C_t v^t divided by sum of C_t v^t. For a zero-coupon bond it equals the term. Modified duration = Macaulay duration / (1 + i)."},
        {q:"61. What is the relationship between duration and coupon?", a:"Higher coupons bring cashflows earlier, so duration falls. Longer term increases duration. Higher yields reduce duration because distant cashflows are discounted more heavily."},
        {q:"62. What is a constant force of mortality assumption?", a:"Assuming mu is constant within each year of age, so t_p_x = (p_x)^t. Alternatives include uniform distribution of deaths (UDD), where t_q_x = t q_x. These are used for fractional ages."},
        {q:"63. What is the complete expectation of life?", a:"e-circle_x = integral from 0 to infinity of t_p_x dt, the average future lifetime. The curtate expectation e_x = sum of k_p_x counts only complete years. Approximately e-circle_x = e_x + 0.5."},
        {q:"64. What is select mortality?", a:"Mortality depending on both age and duration since entry, e.g. since underwriting. Recently selected lives have lighter mortality because they passed medical checks; this effect wears off after the select period, when ultimate rates apply."},
        {q:"65. What causes mortality profit or loss?", a:"Mortality profit = expected death strain - actual death strain. If fewer people die than expected on policies with positive death strain, the insurer profits. For annuities the death strain is negative, so lighter mortality causes a loss."},
        {q:"66. What is a with-profits policy and how are bonuses distributed?", a:"A policy sharing in the insurer's surplus. Bonuses can be reversionary (added to the sum assured each year, simple or compound) or terminal (paid at claim). The insurer smooths returns and must set bonus rates to remain solvent."},
        {q:"67. What is zeroisation in unit-linked profit testing?", a:"Setting up non-unit reserves in earlier years to eliminate future negative cashflows, so the policy is self-financing. This delays profit emergence but ensures losses do not arise in later years."},
        {q:"68. What is the risk discount rate?", a:"The rate used to discount a profit signature. It equals the risk-free rate plus a margin for the risk of the product and the cost of capital. A higher RDR reduces NPV and penalises products with profits emerging late."},
        {q:"69. What is the expected value and variance of a stochastic accumulation?", a:"If annual returns i_t are iid with mean j and variance s^2, E(S_n) = (1 + j)^n. E(S_n^2) = (1 + 2j + j^2 + s^2)^n, from which Var(S_n) follows. Lognormal models give closed-form probabilities."},
        {q:"70. What is the lognormal model for interest rates?", a:"Assume ln(1 + i_t) is normal with mean mu and variance sigma^2, independently each year. Then ln S_n is normal with mean n mu and variance n sigma^2, so S_n is lognormal. It allows probabilities such as the chance a fund falls below a target."},
        {q:"71. What are the main risks of investing in a project?", a:"Uncertainty in cashflows, costs and timing; interest rate and financing risk; inflation; regulatory and political risk; market demand. Handle through sensitivity analysis, scenario testing, stochastic modelling and a higher risk discount rate."},
        {q:"72. What is sensitivity analysis in project appraisal?", a:"Changing one assumption at a time (e.g. costs +10%, revenue -10%) to see the effect on NPV. It shows which assumptions matter most. Scenario analysis changes several assumptions together; stochastic modelling simulates many outcomes."},
        {q:"73. Explain the concept of a sinking fund.", a:"A separate fund into which the borrower pays regularly to accumulate enough to repay the loan capital at the end. Interest on the loan is paid separately. Sinking fund payments = L / s_n at the fund's interest rate."},
        {q:"74. What is APR?", a:"Annual percentage rate of charge: the effective annual rate of interest on a consumer loan including fees, required by law in the UK for comparison. It is found from an equation of value equating the loan with all repayments and charges."},
        {q:"75. What is the difference between real and nominal interest rates?", a:"Nominal rates are actual money rates; real rates remove inflation. Real rates matter for long-term planning and inflation-linked liabilities. Index-linked gilt yields give a market estimate of real rates, and the difference from conventional gilt yields gives implied inflation."}
      ]},
      { name: "Tier 4: Practical, scenario and applied questions", items: [
        {q:"76. How would you explain discounting to a non-actuary?", a:"A pound received in five years is worth less than a pound today because today's pound could be invested. Discounting converts future amounts into today's money so we can compare and add them fairly."},
        {q:"77. Why do GI insurers discount reserves under IFRS 17 and Solvency II?", a:"Claims are paid in the future, and insurers earn investment income while holding the money. Discounting reflects the time value of money and gives a market-consistent value of liabilities, making balance sheets comparable. It adds sensitivity to interest rate changes."},
        {q:"78. How do interest rates affect a GI insurer?", a:"Higher rates increase investment income and reduce the discounted value of reserves, but fall the market value of bond holdings. Long-tail lines are most affected. Pricing may include an investment income allowance, so rate changes feed into premiums."},
        {q:"79. How would you choose a discount rate for valuing liabilities?", a:"Usually a risk-free rate (e.g. a gilt or swap curve, as in the PRA's prescribed Solvency II curve) possibly with an illiquidity or matching adjustment. The rate should reflect the term, currency and nature of liabilities and regulatory requirements."},
        {q:"80. Why might an insurer deliberately mismatch assets and liabilities?", a:"To seek higher expected returns, e.g. holding equities or longer bonds than liabilities. This increases risk and capital requirements under Solvency II, so it requires sufficient free assets and a risk appetite to support it."},
        {q:"81. How would you assess an investment project for a company?", a:"Estimate cashflows and timing, choose a risk discount rate, calculate NPV, IRR and discounted payback period, then do sensitivity and scenario testing. Consider non-financial factors and financing. Recommend if NPV is positive and risks are acceptable."},
        {q:"82. A project has two IRRs. What does that mean and what do you do?", a:"The cashflows change sign more than once, so the NPV crosses zero twice. IRR is then unreliable. Use NPV at the cost of capital instead, or a modified IRR."},
        {q:"83. How would you explain duration to an underwriter?", a:"It tells us roughly how long, on average, until we get our money back, and how much a portfolio's value changes when interest rates move. A duration of 5 means a 1% rise in rates cuts the value by about 5%."},
        {q:"84. How does inflation affect GI claims and reserves?", a:"Claims inflation (wages, parts, court awards) increases future payments, especially in long-tail lines. Reserves must project inflation explicitly; under-allowing causes reserve deficiencies. Recent high inflation in motor repair costs is a good example."},
        {q:"85. What is the difference between CM1 Paper A and Paper B?", a:"Paper A is the written exam on theory and calculations. Paper B is a computer-based assessment in Excel applying the same techniques, such as building loan schedules, profit tests and project appraisals in a spreadsheet."},
        {q:"86. How have you used CM1 in Excel?", a:"Give a concrete example, such as building a cashflow projection or loan schedule with formulas for interest, capital repaid and outstanding balance, or a profit test with a profit signature and NPV. Mention checking results and documenting assumptions."},
        {q:"87. What is a cashflow projection and why do actuaries use it?", a:"A year-by-year forecast of expected incomes and outgoes. It is used for pricing, reserving, capital setting and business planning. It allows timing, inflation, discounting and stress testing to be built in."},
        {q:"88. How do you value a cashflow stream with uncertain timing?", a:"Use expected cashflows with probabilities (e.g. survival or payout pattern) and discount at an appropriate rate. In GI, claim payment patterns from run-off triangles determine the timing used for discounting."},
        {q:"89. Why is liquidity important for insurers?", a:"Insurers must pay claims when due, sometimes in large amounts after catastrophes. Holding enough liquid assets avoids forced sales at poor prices. Short-tail GI insurers need high liquidity."},
        {q:"90. How would you explain the yield curve to a client?", a:"It shows what interest rate you earn for lending money for different lengths of time. Normally longer loans earn more because of the extra risk and uncertainty."},
        {q:"91. What is the impact of a rise in interest rates on a life insurer's annuity book?", a:"The present value of annuity liabilities falls. If assets are well matched, asset values fall by a similar amount. Any mismatch creates a surplus or deficit. Higher rates also make new annuities cheaper for customers."},
        {q:"92. Why are life and GI reserves different in nature?", a:"Life reserves reflect long-term contractual cashflows predicted using mortality and interest. GI reserves cover claims already incurred but not settled (outstanding and IBNR) plus unexpired risk, mostly short to medium term and estimated from claims development data."},
        {q:"93. What is the link between CM1 and CM2?", a:"CM1 provides deterministic financial maths and life contingencies. CM2 extends to stochastic models: utility theory, portfolio theory, asset pricing models, option pricing (Black-Scholes) and ruin theory."},
        {q:"94. What is the difference between a deterministic and stochastic approach?", a:"Deterministic uses single best-estimate assumptions and gives one result. Stochastic treats assumptions as random and gives a distribution of outcomes, showing risk and tail events. Stochastic is more informative but complex and model-dependent."},
        {q:"95. Which CM1 topic did you find hardest and how did you overcome it?", a:"Be honest, e.g. profit testing or immunisation conditions. Describe practising questions, building spreadsheets to see how cashflows behave and understanding the intuition rather than memorising formulas."},
        {q:"96. Describe a time you applied CM1 techniques in a project.", a:"Use a real example from your MSc or your startups: e.g. evaluating a business decision with NPV, building a cashflow model in Excel, or discounting future costs. Use aim, method, result, learning."},
        {q:"97. How would you check a cashflow model is correct?", a:"Reconcile totals, check signs and timing, test simple cases with known answers (e.g. zero interest), compare with an independent calculation, review sensitivity results for sense, and have a peer review."},
        {q:"98. What would you consider when advising on investment strategy for a GI insurer?", a:"Liability term, currency and nature; liquidity needs; regulatory capital (Solvency II); risk appetite; expected returns; diversification; tax; and size of free assets. The core is matching with a controlled amount of mismatching."},
        {q:"99. How would you value a stream of payments growing with inflation?", a:"Either project inflated cashflows and discount at the nominal rate, or discount the unindexed cashflows at the real rate (1 + i)/(1 + j) - 1. Both give the same answer if done consistently."},
        {q:"100. If you could explain one CM1 idea that matters most in your future job, what would it be?", a:"The time value of money, applied through discounting and asset-liability matching. It drives how reserves are valued, how investment income is used in pricing and how an insurer manages interest rate risk."}
      ]}
    ]
  }
};

export default INTERVIEW_BANKS;
